f 17592186045417] [17592186229156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229156 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229156 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229156 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229156 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1039 "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229157 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229255 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229255 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229255 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229255 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229255 #edb/ref 1045 "Using the Trapezoidal Method With Newton's Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229255 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186230032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229255 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229255 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ad098e4e-ddaa-5a8c-8676-9f6c2a1554d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230032 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230032 #edb/ref 1061 594484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230032 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c9d3bf2-ca7c-52cb-b895-adf1cf0cbed3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230032 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230033 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230033 #edb/ref 1061 594485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230033 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33217370-2caf-5db2-a016-cc53daad6665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230033 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230034 #edb/ref 1061 594488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230034 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95fba8e6-749f-55be-9019-f637bd866173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230034 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230186 #edb/ref 1064 "We know how to apply the trapezoidal method to initial value problems that are linear in $y.$ Can we apply it to more complex initial value problems, such as\n\n$$\ny' = 2x^2 - y^4, \\qquad y(0)=1?\n$$\n\nYes, we can. To do so, we define a function $g$ such that the next $y$-value, $y_\\text{new},$ satisfies the equation $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$ Then, we apply a root-finding method to solve for $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's apply the trapezoidal method to the IVP above with step size $\\Delta x = 0.5.$\n\nWe begin as usual. In general, the change $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}^{2}-0.25{y}^{4}+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the update formula for $\\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}^{2}-0.25{y}^{4}+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, there is no simple algebraic rearrangement to isolate $y_\\text{new},$ so solving exactly is impractical.\nInstead, we define a new function $g$ of $y_\\text{new}$ by the expression on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2\n$$\n\nNote that $x,$ $y,$ and $x_\\text{new}$ are known constants at each step, so $g$ is truly a one-variable function in $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nThen, the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a root of this function: $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ Therefore, to find $y_\\text{new},$ we apply a root-finding method, such as Newton's method.\n\nFor instance, in the first step, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 0 + 0.5 = 0.5.$ So, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25\\cdot (1)^{4}-1-0.5\\cdot (0)^{2}-0.5\\cdot (0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we would proceed to use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230186 #edb/ref 1065 14495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230186 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9cd9bd37-323b-5d8a-8d1f-4d8924a59da0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230186 #edb/ref 